Another way to approach this is to perform a slightly more expensive calculation for refraction - we refract the view vector around the normal vector with some known IOR (eta value), then we calculate where this refracted vector (when added to the view vector) would "hit" the background - in other words, we recover our refracted screen UV from the view vector.

I think the code should look roughly like this, but I don't think it's giving me the result I expect and I'm not really sure why. It seems like the screen UVs are being sampled outside the [0.0, 1.0] range...

```glsl
vec2 view_to_uv(vec3 view, mat4 projection_matrix) {
	vec4 new_view_clip = (projection_matrix * vec4(view, 0.0));
	vec3 new_view_ndc = new_view_clip.xyz / new_view_clip.w;
	return vec2(new_view_ndc.x, new_view_ndc.y) * 0.5 + 0.5;
}

float eta = 1.5; // glass

vec3 refracted = refract(-VIEW, ref_normal, eta);

if (refracted == vec3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)) {
	// total internal reflection
} else {
	// add -VIEW to refracted view vector
	refracted -= VIEW;

	// find new screen uv
	vec2 new_screen_uv = view_to_uv(refracted_view, PROJECTION_MATRIX);

	// emit
	EMISSION += texture(screen_texture, new_screen_uv).rgb;
}
```

In any case, do you think this method has merit over simply taking the xy component of the view space normals?
